"Term: Incunabula | RBMS Controlled Vocabularies Community Discussion (https://rbms.info/cv-comments/2015/02/21/term-incunabula/)\nciteturn11search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11.7 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; Use for books printed with movable metal type before 1501. ... Approved terms are available at http://rbms.info/vocabularies/index.shtml. ... Leslie’s comment of January 27, 2015: “‘Incunabula’ is a printing term; it doesn’t fit the scope of Genre terms.”\n# RBMS Controlled Vocabularies Community Discussion\n\n### RBMS Controlled Vocabularies Community Discussion\n\n# Term: Incunabula\n\n  * Approved\n  * New Terms\n  * Printing Evidence\nFebruary 21, 2015\n\nTerm:\nIncunabula\n\n\nThesaurus:\nPrinting evidence\n\n* * *\n\nScope note:\nUse for books printed with movable metal type before 1501.\n\n* * *\n\nUse for:\nCradle books\nFifteeners\nIncunables\n\n* * *\n\nThe discussion period for this term was from March 9, 2015 to March 16, 2015. The discussion period for this term is now closed. Committee discussion to date can be found here. Approved terms are available at http://rbms.info/vocabularies/index.shtml.\n\nAuthors\n\nImage Rare Books and Manuscripts Section\n\n### 2 Comments\n\n  1. Imageamyfbrown - March 9, 2015 said:\n\nComment from DCRM-L on 2/14/14: Could the word “books” be changed to something more generic, such as “resources” or expanded to something like “books and other materials”? Some atlases were printed in the 15th century, and do appear in standard bibliographies of incunabula, but they are considered cartographic materials, not really “books”. A broader term would also better cover pamphlets and broadsides.\n\nAlso, the scope note could be read as implying that the entire item needs to have been printed from moveable metal type. I wonder if it needs to be finessed to account for the fact that some parts of the resource (plates) may have been printed by other means.\n\nMaybe something like:\n\nSN: Use for books and other materials printed wholly or partly with movable metal type before 1501.\n\n  2. ImageChristopher D. Cook - March 9, 2015 said:\n\nI respectfully disagree with Deborah J. Leslie’s comment of January 27, 2015: “‘Incunabula’ is a printing term; it doesn’t fit the scope of Genre terms.” I believe the opposite is true. According to the introduction of Printing and Publishing Evidence, that thesaurus is used to “provide standard terms for the retrieval of physical evidence of printing and publishing practices.” The term “incunabula” belongs in Genre Terms because it is an intellectual concept, not a term for physical evidence.\n\nComments are closed.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFebruary | 2015 | RBMS Controlled Vocabularies Community Discussion (https://rbms.info/cv-comments/2015/02/)\nciteturn11search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 months ago; # RBMS Controlled Vocabularies Community Discussion ... Term: Incunabula Thesaurus: Printing evidence Scope note: Use for books printed with movable metal type before 1501.\n# RBMS Controlled Vocabularies Community Discussion\n\n### RBMS Controlled Vocabularies Community Discussion\n\n# Monthly Archives: February 2015\n\n## Term: Thumb Indexes\n\nRare Books and Manuscripts Section - February 21, 2015 - Approved, Binding Terms, New Terms\n\nTerm: Thumb indexes Thesaurus: Binding terms Scope note: Use for cut-outs on the fore-edge of a book intended for locating sections within the volume, especially common on dictionaries and Bibles Use for: Cuts, thumb Cut-in indexes Gouge indexes Indexes, cut-in Indexes, gouge Indexes, thumb Thumb cuts Hierarchy: Text block – Edges of text block The…\n\n## Term: Registers\n\nRare Books and Manuscripts Section - February 21, 2015 - Approved, Binding Terms, New Terms\n\nTerm: Registers Thesaurus: Binding terms Scope Note: Use for ribbons attached at the head of the spine to be used as a place marker in the book. For unattached devices intended to be inserted 